{"parentUuid":"721027a3-0486-4d20-af82-8ce51187325e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdNmpaU68biejvQF19tK5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking for a requirements document to transform LONGVIEW into a next-generation self-learning system. They want something they can review and then hand off to another session for design and implementation. The core requirements include bi-temporal data modeling to track both when something was true and when it was recorded, plus an additive-only approach that never deletes—just appends and moves data around, which LONGVIEW already supports through its teleport mechanism.\n\nBeyond that, the system needs delta-driven incremental processing, orchestration and execution tagged with configuration details and performance metrics, autonomous agents running in optimization loops, and the ability to learn and improve as it expands. It should be multi-machine aware, with raw data staging on a portable drive for true plug-and-play portability, and context-aware across machines, processes, and knowledge domains.\n\nThis requirements doc ties together the existing LONGVIEW session-synthesis pipeline, the EP-T training pipeline that turns sessions into improved models via QLoRA and DPO, the delivery board for task and agent management, and the multi-machine reality of running trainers and inference across different hardware.\n\nThe core loop is: sessions generate data across machines → portable drive stages raw data → LONGVIEW synthesizes it with bi-temporal and delta processing → datasets get built → models train → models serve inference → agents use those models to do work → more sessions happen → the cycle repeats and compounds. I need to write a clear, reviewable requirements document grounded in what's already been built, focusing on functional and non-functional requirements rather than solution design or task breakdown. LONGVIEW synthesizes