lid_time (when something actually happened in the world) and transaction_time (when the system recorded it) — which is essential for a learning system that needs to audit and replay the exact knowledge state a model was trained on.\n\nThen there's the append-only, additive nature of the system: corrections become new versions rather than overwrites, nothing gets deleted, which preserves full history and reproducibility. The delta-driven approach extends LONGVIEW's existing `--delta` capability with content-addressed hashing and per-source watermarks, so only new or changed sessions get processed — this is what enables scaling as sessions accumulate across machines.\n\nFinally, every operation (synthesis, training, eval, serving) becomes a first-class execution record tagged with its config, model version, hardware details, and other metadata for full traceability. The portable drive becomes self-describing when attached to any machine, automatically resuming the pipeline with full context about which machine, process, and project generated each session. This creates a flywheel where sessions feed into LONGVIEW synthesis, which builds knowledge artifacts and training data that improve the model, which agents then use to generate better sessions—each iteration tracked and measured to demonstrate compound improvement over time.\n\nNow I'm drafting the requirements document with numbered SHALL statements organized by theme and assigned requirement IDs, and I need to think through whether we should stage raw data on the portable drive as a requirement versus a recommendation, plus flag the exact staging format as an open design question. I'll write this to the architecture directory as a comprehensive but scannable doc that maps back to what we've already built.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":660867,"cache_read_input_tokens":24853,"output_tokens":4460,"server_tool